      "solution": "use strict;\nuse bigint;\n\nchomp(my $input = <STDIN>);\nmy $n = Math::BigInt->new($input);\n\nwhile (1) {\n    my $s = $n->bstr();\n    my $r = reverse($s);\n    last if $s eq $r;\n    $n->badd($r);\n}\n\nprint $n->bstr().\"\\n\";",
      "statement": "For a non-negative integer [[n]], we can iterate the following process:\n\n1. If [[n]] is a palindrome, use [[n]] as output [[r]] and stop.\n2. Add reverse([[n]]) to [[n]].\n3. Go to step 1.\n\nreverse([[n]]) reverses all digits of [[n]]. {{123456}} becomes {{654321}}. [[n]] is a palindrome if [[n]] equals reverse([[n]]).\n\nGiven [[n]], output the result [[r]] of the iterative process. For the given inputs, the iterative process always terminates.",
